    "_metadata": {
     "name": "Mario Lemieux",
     "description": "In a defining moment of the 1991 Stanley Cup Final against the Minnesota North Stars, Mario Lemieux delivered a goal that would become iconic. This extraordinary play paved the way for the Pittsburgh Penguins to secure their first-ever Stanley Cup victory. With the Penguins holding a 2-1 lead late in the second period, Lemieux takes control of the puck deep in his own zone and swiftly sets off, building momentum as he moves up the ice. Crossing the blueline, Lemieux showcases his unmatched skill by gracefully maneuvering past two Minnesota defenders. With a quick shift of the puck onto his backhand, he expertly places it beyond the reach of goaltender Jon Casey. The goal not only showcased Lemieux's brilliance but also propelled the Penguins closer to their championship triumph. Throughout the playoffs, Lemieux displayed incredible prowess, leading to him being awarded the prestigious Conn Smythe Trophy. His exceptional performance included registering an impressive 44 points in just 23 games, ranking as the second-highest number of playoff points ever recorded in a single season in NHL history.\r\n\r\nOfficially Licensed Product of the NHL, NHLPA, and/or NHLAA. All copyright, logos, and marks are the property of their respective owners and are used, under license, by Sweet. All Rights Reserved. [Terms Apply](https://nhlbreakaway.com/terms).",
